INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (7, 1, 'luv capabilities', 900, 'Part 1 of `plan 1: masterplan`: find out what luv can carry for a solar-system flight game, by building luv and running its programs, tests and screenshot paths, not only by reading. The findings ground `plan 1: masterplan` parts 2 to 5 (skeleton, architecture, design, story), so every claim in the deliverables says how it was checked (ran, read, or assumed).

## Ground rules
- `../luv` is read-only: build and run it from its flake (`nix build`/`nix run` on `github:mbrock/luv` or `path:../luv`), or with build outputs in a temp dir; never edit or commit there, and leave no new files that `git -C ../luv status` would show (its `result` symlink already exists; leave it).
- Note the luv revision examined (`git -C ../luv rev-parse HEAD`) at the top of `docs/luv-capabilities.md`; part 2 pins the same revision in `flake.lock`.
- Every window opened is closed (`./sly stop-playing`, or `kill $!` by PID); no image or game left running; never kill by name pattern.
- Docs style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph, then concise bullets.
- Headless/no-display findings matter: record what works without a display (agents run in such sessions).

## Reading list (in `../luv`)
- `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `Makefile`, `flake.nix` (packages, apps, devShells, `applicationPackage`, the luv nix cache), `luv.asd`, `luft.asd`, `luvcraft.asd` (system names and dependencies).
- `wiki/`: `index.org`, `gpu-architecture.org`, `metal-backend.org`, `mathematical-shaders.org`, `sky-and-light.org`, `temporal-antialiasing.org`, `frame-performance.org`, `physics-and-simd.org`, `sb-simd.org`, `quantities-and-measurement.org`, `field-notes-measures.org`, `world-terminal.org`, `slug-bezier.org` (text), `technical-debt.org`, `luft-star-figures.org` (stars), `application-workbench.org`.
- Code: `hal/` (`gpu.lisp`, `canvas.lisp`, `application-lifecycle.lisp`, `application-capture.lisp`, `capture-specification.lisp`, `png.lisp`, `sdl/`, `vulkan/`, `metal/`, `shader/`), `luv/`, `terminal/`, `arithmetic/`, `luft/star-*.lisp`, `luvcraft/` (game loop, input, physics, sky), `scripts/` (`test.lisp`, `captures.lisp`, `readme-screenshots.lisp`, `build-application`), `sly`, `sly-init.lisp`.

## Run (each run noted in the doc with command, time, outcome)
- `nix run --accept-flake-config github:mbrock/luv` (Luvcraft) and `#luft`: does a window open here, how fast from the cache, what input it takes; close it.
- `nix build` of `#luvcraft`, `#luft`, `#luv-shaderc`; `luv-shaderc` on `hal/shaderc/examples/sky.lisp` into a temp dir.
- The test suite: `make test` / `make test-suite` / `make luft-test` inside `nix develop ../luv`, outputs kept out of `../luv` where possible; if a target can only write into `../luv/build` (ignored build output), note it.
- The live image: `./sly start`, `./sly play luft`, `./sly status`, `./sly screenshot /tmp/…png` (read the PNG), `./sly eval`, redefining a shader or function while playing, `./sly stop-playing`, stopping the image by PID. Find out whether `./sly` can serve a different checkout (this repo) or how part 2 should copy the setup.
- Screenshots and headless: `scripts/captures`, `hal/application-capture.lisp`: can a frame be rendered offscreen to PNG without a window or display (for tests and the screenshot command)?
- A tiny probe program loaded from a temp dir against luv''s ASDF systems: open a window, draw a sphere mesh with a Lisp shader, take a screenshot, close. This answers which systems a game loads (`luv`, `luv/shader`, `luv/terminal`, `luv/arithmetic`, …) and seeds part 2 (walking skeleton); keep its source in the doc or an appendix, not as repo code.

## Capabilities to report
- Rendering: meshes and vertex formats, Lisp shaders (stages, uniforms, textures, compute), lighting, shadows, sky/sun, bloom, fog, TAA, in-world text (Slug glyphs), terminal screens (Ghostty) and whether they can show game-generated text (instrument panels) without a shell.
- Windowing and input: SDL3, keyboard, mouse (relative mode), gamepad/joystick (SDL3 has it; does luv expose it?), resizing, fullscreen.
- Live image and dev loop: `./sly`, Swash, failure parking and `./sly resume`.
- Screenshots and headless runs, tests (Parachute), how luv runs its own tests.
- Physics and SIMD: what luvcraft''s physics is (voxel collision, not orbital), `sb-simd`, use of `double-float`; the quantities/units library and whether it fits SI orbital mechanics.
- Packaging: `applicationPackage` in luv''s flake, whether this repo can reuse it through the luv flake input (`lib`, overlays) or must copy it; the binary cache (`luv.swa.sh`).
- Platforms: Linux x86-64/ARM64 (Vulkan), macOS Apple Silicon (Metal 4); what was tested on this machine vs. only read.
- Performance: frame budget numbers from `frame-performance.org` or measured.

## Gaps to assess, each with a workaround in this repo
- Depth range across 10^12 m: reversed-Z with float depth, logarithmic depth, or layered passes (far bodies, near bodies, cockpit); check what depth format and clear luv uses and whether a game can set them.
- Double-precision positions: physics in `double-float` in Lisp, camera-relative single-float positions sent to the GPU.
- Spheres and planet surfaces: UV/icosphere generation, an Earth shader (texture vs. procedural continents, clouds, atmosphere rim), texture loading (which image formats luv loads; licences of public Earth textures, e.g. NASA Blue Marble).
- Starfield: luft''s star tables, or a catalogue (e.g. HYG, Yale BSC; licence noted) drawn as points or sprites.
- Sun glare and brightness: bloom from luvcraft''s sky, a sun disc, exposure across Earth-lit and deep-space scenes.
- Orbit lines and markers: does luv draw lines? otherwise thin quads or ribbons.
- HUD/2D overlay: screen-space text and shapes over the 3D view, vs. everything drawn on in-world cockpit screens.
- Audio: luv''s main shell has none (`sdl3-mixer` only in a side shell); SDL3 audio via cl-sdl3, or cut.
- Save files: luvcraft''s save path; a small s-expression save under `$XDG_DATA_HOME`.
- Anything else found (e.g. fixed physics step decoupled from frame rate, instancing many objects).

## Deliverables
- `docs/luv-capabilities.md`: luv revision, one section per capability area (what it offers, how it was checked, limits), the gaps each with its workaround, and a short "what this means for luv-in-space" summary that parts 2 to 4 of `plan 1: masterplan` build on.
- `docs/luv-wishlist.md`: what would better live in luv than here (e.g. a reversed-Z option, double-precision camera helpers, sphere meshes, line rendering, audio), each with why.
- Decisions recorded with `pm decision record`, at least: which luv ASDF systems the game depends on; how the game consumes luv (flake input, which package or lib output); the depth strategy and the precision approach as findings-backed recommendations (part 3, architecture, may supersede them); which platforms are targeted and tested.
- Follow-up tasks (`pm task add`) for gaps needing work outside this part, in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- Read and summarise luv''s docs and systems (no running).
- Build and run luv''s programs, tests, live image and screenshots; record outcomes.
- Probe program: a sphere in a window plus a screenshot, from a temp dir against luv.
- Assess the gaps with workarounds; write `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/luv-wishlist.md`; record the decisions.', '2026-10-14T16:13:00Z', 8);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (8, 1, 'walking skeleton', 800, 'Part 2 of `plan 1: masterplan`: an ASDF system in this repo, built against luv pinned as a flake input, that opens a window and draws something recognisable, with a headless test and a screenshot command an agent can use, and `AGENTS.md` "Commands" filled in. It runs after `plan 7: luv capabilities` (priority 900) and builds on its findings and decisions: which luv ASDF systems to load, how a luv program is built and started (`applicationPackage`, `scripts/build-application`), how screenshots and headless runs work (`hal/application-capture.lisp`, `./sly screenshot`), and its probe program (sphere in a window plus a screenshot), which seeds this skeleton.

## Ground rules
- Read `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and the decisions of `plan 7: luv capabilities` first (`pm decision list --answered --grep luv --grep depth --grep platform`); they are binding unless superseded.
- `../luv` is read-only reference; the build uses luv from the flake input only, at the revision noted in `docs/luv-capabilities.md`.
- Every window opened is closed (by PID or `./sly stop-playing`); no process left running.
- Out of scope: game features. The scene is a placeholder, marked `PLACEHOLDER(<task id>)` in code and on screen.
- Before each commit the build and the test pass.

## Flake
- `flake.nix`: add `luv` as an input (`github:mbrock/luv`, pinned in `flake.lock`; `nixpkgs.follows` only if luv tolerates it, else keep luv''s nixpkgs for its cache); `nixConfig` with luv''s binary cache (`luv.swa.sh`) as luv''s flake does.
- `packages.default` (`luv-in-space`): built with luv''s `applicationPackage` if the input exposes it (`lib` or similar), else a copy adapted here, noted in `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.
- `apps.default`: starts the game (`nix run` from the repo root).
- `devShells.default`: luv''s dev shell environment (SBCL with luv''s Lisp deps, Vulkan/SDL3 libraries) plus `pm` and `git`; `direnv` (`.envrc` with `use flake`) keeps working.
- `checks.<system>.default`: the headless test, if it can run inside the nix sandbox (no GPU); otherwise the test is a dev-shell command and the reason is noted.
- Platforms: those decided in `plan 7: luv capabilities`; tested on this machine (Linux x86-64), others only evaluated (`nix flake check --no-build` or `nix eval`).

## ASDF system
- `luv-in-space.asd` at the repo root: system `luv-in-space` (sources under `src/`), `luv-in-space/tests` (under `tests/`, Parachute as luv uses it).
- `src/package.lisp`, `src/main.lisp` (entry point: open a window, run the frame loop, quit on Escape or window close), `src/scene.lisp` (the placeholder scene).
- The scene: a lit sphere (Earth placeholder, procedural colour, no texture) against a dark background with a few points or a sun direction; a visible `PLACEHOLDER(<task id>)` label (in-world text or terminal screen, whatever `plan 7: luv capabilities` found cheapest), the task id being the task that builds the scene.
- Shaders written in Lisp the way luv''s programs do; nothing that pre-empts the depth and precision decisions of part 3 (architecture), beyond what the probe already did.

## Test
- A headless test command (e.g. `make test` or `scripts/test`, run inside the dev shell): loads `luv-in-space/tests` and runs it with a non-zero exit on failure.
- Tests: the system loads; a pure function (e.g. sphere mesh generation: vertex count, unit radius) is correct; if luv supports offscreen rendering without a display, one frame renders to a PNG with the expected size and non-background pixels at the sphere''s centre.

## Screenshot
- A screenshot command (e.g. `scripts/screenshot out.png`): starts the game, renders some frames, writes a PNG, exits; works for an agent (from a terminal, without a human). Offscreen if luv allows it, else in a window that it closes itself.
- Optional `--frames N` or a time argument; the default shows the scene.

## Live image
- If `./sly` (luv''s Slynk-based live image) can load this repo''s system (from `../luv` or via the flake''s dev shell), a `./sly`-like wrapper here or documented steps: start the image, load `luv-in-space`, play, screenshot, redefine a function while playing, stop. If it can''t be reused, say why in `AGENTS.md` and add a follow-up task.

## AGENTS.md "Commands"
- Fill in: dev shell, build (`nix build`), run (`nix run`), test, screenshot, live image; each command one bullet.
- Verify from a clean checkout: `git clone` into a temp dir, run each command there, delete the clone; note the time a cold build takes.
- Record a decision for the lasting choices (`pm decision record`): the layout of systems and directories, the test and screenshot commands.

## Suggested split
- Flake: luv input, dev shell, package and `apps.default` building an empty system that opens a window.
- The placeholder scene with its `PLACEHOLDER` label.
- Headless test command and the screenshot command.
- Live-image dev loop (or the reason it''s skipped).
- `AGENTS.md` "Commands", verified from a clean clone; decisions recorded.', '2026-10-14T16:13:35Z', 9);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (9, 1, 'architecture', 700, 'Part 3 of `plan 1: masterplan`: write `docs/architecture.md`, the technical ground every later milestone builds on. It runs after `plan 7: luv capabilities` and `plan 8: walking skeleton` and rests on `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, the skeleton in `src/`, `tests/`, `scripts/`, `flake.nix`, and the decisions they recorded. It writes down how the game is built on luv and the technical choices that are still open (units, frames, integrator, time warp, module layout, testing), and records those choices as decisions so the design (`task 11: write plan: game design`) and `milestone 2: Flight core: Newtonian flight in near-Earth orbit` can implement from it.

## Ground rules
- Read first: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` (especially "Gaps and workarounds" and "What this means for luv-in-space"), `docs/luv-wishlist.md`, `AGENTS.md`, the skeleton''s code, and `pm decision list --answered`.
- Binding decisions, cited in the document, not reopened: `decision 1: How the game consumes luv`, `decision 2: Screenshots and GPU test: offscreen capture on a hidden canvas, headless on lavapipe`, `decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on`, `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 6: Target platforms`, `decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads`, `decision 8: Live image: ./sly wraps luv''s client from the flake input`, `decision 9: Layout of systems and directories`, `decision 10: Test and screenshot commands`, `decision 11: Render pipelines are luv live shader pipelines`.
- The document describes; it does not implement. No game code beyond what a check needs (e.g. a throwaway numeric experiment in `/tmp` to pick an integrator step is fine; it is not committed).
- Where a choice depends on the game design (`docs/design.md`, written later by the plan from `task 11: write plan: game design`), state the architecture''s default and its constraint on the design (e.g. "thrust only below warp x10"), so the design picks within it.
- Style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, then concise bullets; units in SI; cite decisions by id and subject.
- Out of scope: game features; the design and story documents.

## docs/architecture.md: contents
### Dependency on luv
- Flake input pinned in `flake.lock`, dev shell and package from `luv.lib.environmentFor`, the copied application recipe, binary cache, launcher without `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` (`decision 1`).
- ASDF systems: `luv-in-space` depends on `luv` only; tests add `luv/test-support` and Parachute; how a new luv system becomes a dependency (recorded decision first, `decision 3`); candidates already known: `luv/production` for a trajectory-prediction worker, `luv/arithmetic` quantities at boundaries.
- What the game builds on from luv: HAL (devices, pipelines, buffers, textures), the shader language with live shader pipelines (`decision 11`), the SDL3 canvas and its events, `capture-application-screenshot`, `audio-sink`; what it writes itself (camera, projection, impostors, stars, ribbons, text/screens, HDR, physics, audio mixer, saves), each pointing to its section in `docs/luv-capabilities.md`.
- Dev loop: `./sly` live image (start, play, eval to redefine functions and shaders while playing, screenshot, stop), `scripts/test`, `scripts/screenshot`, `nix build`/`nix run`, `nix flake check`; what to keep live-redefinable (no closures captured over functions the loop calls; state in the `game` object).
- luv wishlist: how a gap moves to `docs/luv-wishlist.md`.

### Module layout
- Target layout of `src/` as files and packages, extending the skeleton (`package`, `shaders`, `scene`, `main`, `capture`) per `decision 9`; one line each on purpose and dependencies, in load order, e.g.:
  - math (double-float vec3/quaternion on the CPU, single-float conversion at the GPU boundary), units/constants (G·M of bodies, radii, J2000 epoch);
  - bodies and ephemerides (on-rails positions of Sun, Earth, Moon, planets as functions of time);
  - physics (state, gravity, integrator, Kepler propagation, orbital elements), ship (rigid body, mass, thrusters, propellant), simulation clock and time warp;
  - rendering: camera and projection, sphere impostors, stars, ribbons/markers, meshes (ship, asteroids), HDR and tone map, the 2D/text pass for instruments;
  - cockpit and instruments, autopilot (orbit targeting, presets), input mapping, audio, story/quests, save/load, main loop, capture;
  - tests mirroring the modules (`tests/<module>.lisp`).
- Package split: one game package plus `luv-in-space.shaders` as now, or more; decide and say why.
- Which milestone first creates each module (2 to 6), consistent with the masterplan''s milestone list.
- Assets: where textures (Blue Marble) and the star table (HYG ≤ 6.5) live, how they are fetched or committed (size limits, Nix fetch vs. git), how attribution and the CC BY-SA table are kept (from `docs/luv-capabilities.md`).

### Units and coordinates
- Unit of length: metre (SI throughout: m, s, kg, N, m/s); state why not km or AU (precision of double over 10^12 m: ~1e-4 m at 1 AU, ample); constants as μ = GM (m^3/s^2) from a named source (e.g. IAU/JPL values).
- Time: simulation time as double seconds since an epoch (e.g. J2000 TDB, or a game-start epoch with a fixed calendar date); resolution of double seconds over decades of game time.
- Frames: the frame of integration (heliocentric inertial, ecliptic J2000? or the dominant body''s inertial frame with re-centring on sphere-of-influence change); planet-centred inertial frames for orbital elements and instruments; body-fixed rotating frame for Earth''s surface (crash detection, ground track, the texture''s longitude); ship-local body frame (x forward, y, z) for thrusters and cockpit; the camera frame. Give the transforms between them and where each is used.
- Rendering: camera-relative positions computed in double, then single float (`decision 5`); the projection function in one place (reversed-Z, infinite far, near 0.1 m, `decision 4`), including the Vulkan clip-y flip; cockpit in the same pass; stars at infinity first.
- Floating-origin pitfalls to name: subtracting large positions before converting, cancelling terms computed in double on the CPU (ray-sphere `h(2r+h)`), orbit-line vertices made relative to the camera each frame.

### Time and integration
- The clock: the frame adds `elapsed x warp` to a double accumulator and runs fixed steps, capped per frame (sim slows rather than stalls); render the latest state or interpolate.
- The integrator for the ship: choose and justify (e.g. symplectic velocity Verlet/leapfrog at a fixed step for coasting and thrust, or RK4/RK45 with step control); step size for LEO (orbital period ~5,500 s) with the error it gives; thrust and attitude (quaternion, angular velocity, torques from RCS) integrated in the same step.
- Gravity model: point masses (Earth, Moon, Sun as the design needs); patched conics vs. n-body for the ship: choose (e.g. n-body with on-rails bodies, the dominant body''s frame for precision; patched conics only for prediction and the autopilot); Earth J2 in or out, with the reason.
- On rails: planets and moons from ephemerides (choose: Keplerian elements with secular rates, e.g. JPL "Approximate Positions of the Planets", vs. a truncated series; the Moon''s model); the ship on rails (Kepler propagation from elements) while coasting at high warp in one body''s field.
- Time warp: levels (e.g. x1 to x100,000 or more), when each is allowed (no thrust above a level; altitude limits near a body; drop out of warp before an SOI change, periapsis below the surface, a scheduled burn); switching between integrated and on-rails motion without a jump (state → elements → state round-trip).
- Collisions: crash detection against body spheres (and terrain height if ever), done per step with a swept test so high warp can''t tunnel; the Sun''s heat as a function of distance (the rule itself is the design''s).
- Trajectory prediction for the map, instruments and the autopilot: where it runs (frame thread with a budget, or a worker via `luv/production`, then a recorded dependency), its horizon and resolution.
- Determinism: same inputs and steps give the same trajectory (for tests and scripted runs).

### Testing
- Headless physics tests as `milestone 2` requires: energy and angular-momentum drift bounds for a circular and an eccentric Earth orbit over N orbits at each warp level and in rails mode; set concrete bounds (e.g. relative energy drift < 1e-6 per 100 orbits; state the numbers the integrator choice makes reachable) and how they''re measured.
- Other tests: Kepler round-trip (state ↔ elements), ephemeris spot checks against JPL Horizons values (a handful, committed as constants with the source), frame transforms, warp transitions without jumps, crash detection, save/load round-trip.
- Scripted runs: driving the game from a script (an input sequence or a control function) for a burn, an orbit raise, a deorbit, each ending with a screenshot (`scripts/screenshot` or `./sly screenshot`); how `milestone 2`''s "Done when" is demonstrated by an agent.
- GPU tests stay as `decision 2`/`decision 10`: one frame on a hidden canvas with lavapipe, image checks rather than pixel-exact comparisons.
- How agents build, run, test and screenshot: point to `AGENTS.md` "Commands" rather than duplicating.

## Decisions to record (`pm decision record`)
- Unit of length (and SI units throughout) and the time epoch.
- Coordinate frames: the frame of integration, re-centring rule, body-fixed frames.
- Integrator and step size; gravity model (n-body on rails vs. patched conics; J2).
- Ephemerides source and model for the bodies.
- Time-warp rules (levels, thrust limit, rails switching).
- Module layout and package split.
- Asset storage and attribution (if decided here rather than left to the design).
- Depth strategy and precision are already `decision 4` and `decision 5`: cite, don''t re-record; supersede only with a reason.

## Done when
- `docs/architecture.md` covers the sections above, agrees with `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, `AGENTS.md` and the skeleton, and cites the decisions it relies on.
- The new decisions are recorded; follow-ups the document reveals (e.g. a missing luv wishlist entry, a test the skeleton lacks) are added as tasks in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- Dependency on luv, dev loop and module layout (incl. assets).
- Units, frames and time; decisions recorded.
- Integration, gravity model, ephemerides, time warp, collisions and prediction; a throwaway numeric check of the integrator''s drift at the chosen step; decisions recorded.
- Testing section with concrete drift bounds; final read-through against the other documents.', '2026-10-14T17:31:46Z', 10);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (10, 1, 'game design', 600, 'Part 4 of `plan 1: masterplan`: write `docs/design.md`, the game design written for implementers. It runs after `plan 9: architecture` and stays inside what `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/architecture.md` say luv and the architecture can carry. `milestone 2: Flight core: Newtonian flight in near-Earth orbit` to `milestone 6: Release: one command to play, a README for people` each implement "what `docs/design.md` assigns" to them, so the document''s scope list is their specification; `task 12: write plan: story` writes `docs/story.md` on top of it and may use only features the scope list puts in milestones 2 to 5.

## Ground rules
- Read first: `docs/luv-capabilities.md` ("Gaps and workarounds", "What this means for luv-in-space"), `docs/architecture.md` (all of it; "Time and integration" leaves choices to the design), `docs/luv-wishlist.md`, `AGENTS.md`, the milestones (`pm milestone show 2` … `6`) and `pm decision list --answered`.
- Binding, cited, not reopened: `decision 4: Depth strategy`, `decision 5: Precision of positions`, `decision 6: Target platforms`, `decision 7: Placeholder labels: dot-matrix overlay quads`, `decision 12: Module layout and package split`, `decision 13: Asset storage and attribution`, `decision 14: Units and time epoch`, `decision 15: Coordinate frames`, `decision 16: Integrator and simulation clock`, `decision 17: Gravity model`, `decision 18: Ephemerides`, `decision 19: Time-warp rules`, `decision 20: Trajectory prediction on the frame thread`, `decision 21: Physics test bounds and flight scripts`. Where the architecture offers the design a choice within limits (thrust limit under warp, crash radii, SOI drop-out, burn lead time, the Sun''s rule, drag, audio''s milestone, Ghostty, gamepad), the design picks within them and says so; going beyond a limit needs a superseding decision with the reason.
- Realism over spectacle, scope over realism (`milestone 1`): a feature luv can''t carry, or that costs far more than it shows, is cut with the reason.
- Numbers in SI with display units in brackets where they help (km, km/s, t); every physical figure (masses, thrust, Isp, Δv, orbits) is computed, not guessed: state the formula or source next to it. A throwaway calculation in `/tmp` is fine; nothing of it is committed.
- Real data with sources named (title, publisher or URL, date read): orbital elements of stations or asteroids, engine figures, body data. Bodies'' μ and radii come from `docs/architecture.md` "Units" (`decision 14`), not new sources.
- Names of things in code and docs are consistent across the document (module names as in `docs/architecture.md` "Files in load order").
- Style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, then concise bullets; tables where they help (ship parts, places, scope list).
- The document describes; no game code. Out of scope: the story itself (`docs/story.md`), beyond what the design needs to name (e.g. that quests exist and how messages reach the cockpit).

## docs/design.md: contents
### The game in one page
- What the player does, minute to minute and hour to hour; the start (the ship in LEO, the date per `decision 14`, final choice with the story); what makes it a showcase of luv (live shaders, Lisp, a real Earth).
- Pillars (e.g. the ship is a tool, Newton is the game, the cockpit is home-made) used later to justify cuts.

### The ship(s)
- The player''s ship: a rugged DIY tug. Parts as a table: steel truss frame, life-support module (crew 1, maybe 2), propellant tanks, main engine(s), RCS thruster blocks, grapple/docking gear (if assigned), radiators, solar panels or other power, batteries; each with mass (kg), dimensions (m), position in ship body axes (FRD, `decision 15`).
- Propulsion: choose realistic, near-future technology (e.g. a storable or methalox chemical main engine, cold gas or hydrazine RCS, maybe a separate electric drive for slow tug work): thrust (N), Isp (s), propellant and its density, tank volumes; sourced from real engines of the class.
- Mass budget and Δv: dry mass, propellant, wet mass, Δv by the rocket equation (Δv = Isp g₀ ln(m₀/m₁)) empty and with a towed load (e.g. a 50 t asteroid); thrust-to-weight and burn times for typical burns (LEO raise, TLI, capture); check against the Δv each place in "The places" needs, so every destination in scope is reachable (with refuelling where needed) and the story can rely on it.
- Inertia: the principal moments (kg m²) from the parts (`decision 16` integrates them), how they change with propellant; RCS torques and translation forces; resulting rotation and translation accelerations, so manual control feels right.
- Refuelling and resupply: where (depots in "The places"), how (docked and a key, or as designed), cost if any (an economy or none, say which).
- Damage: what can be damaged (by collision speed with stations/asteroids, by heat near the Sun, by over-stress while towing?), its effects (lost thruster, leak, dead screen), repair (at a station, or none); keep it minimal and say what is cut.
- Other ships (traffic, the story''s characters): which kinds exist, built from the same parts; whether they move on rails only (`decision 19`).
- How they look: built from simple generated meshes (`meshes.lisp`: tubes, boxes, cylinders, spheres), materials (bare steel, foil, painted markings), lights; recognisable silhouettes, function obvious at a glance; a sketch per ship as a parts list with dimensions.

### Flight
- Controls: a complete key map (keyboard and mouse, luv''s canvas events) for attitude (pitch, yaw, roll), translation (RCS), throttle and engine cut, attitude holds (prograde, retrograde, normal, anti-normal, radial in/out, target, inertial), warp up/down, views, map, autopilot, pause, quit; mouse for looking around in the cockpit and pressing switches; gamepad: assign a milestone or cut (`docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Gamepad and joystick").
- Control modes: direct RCS (rate) vs. rate damping (SAS-like stabiliser, a realistic flight computer function), holds; what the player feels at x1 and x4 (fine step).
- Views: cockpit (eye point, head look), outside chase/orbit camera, map/orbit view (Earth or the current body with orbit lines, the prediction ribbon, targets, at which zoom); what each shows and when it is available; how the camera handles 10^12 m (zoom range, focus body).
- Instruments: the list (altitude, speed, apoapsis/periapsis, inclination, period, attitude ball or equivalent, propellant and Δv remaining, throttle, warp actual/nominal, clock and date, target distance and closing speed, heat, alarms); which milestone each comes in (the minimal readout of `milestone 2`, the full set of `milestone 3`).
- Time warp: the levels the game offers (from `decision 19`), the thrust limit under warp (≤ x1,000), SOI drop-out (stop or continue), burn lead time (default 60 s), how warp is shown and refused.
- The autopilot: what it can do per milestone: circularise, raise/lower apoapsis or periapsis, change plane, rendezvous with a point of interest and stop at a set range (`milestone 3`), transfers (TLI, Hohmann to an asteroid or planet, capture; `milestone 4`); how the player picks a preset, sees the planned burns (on the map and the screens), watches and interrupts them; safety rules (never plans a periapsis below the crash radius; aborts on low propellant); it writes `controls` like the player (`milestone 3` constraint: same physics, same fuel).
- Close-range manoeuvring: docking or grappling: which one, how it''s flown (RCS translation, a docking camera/range finder), tolerances (closing speed, alignment), what happens on contact; assigned to a milestone or cut.
- Towing: attaching to an asteroid or cargo, the combined mass and inertia, how thrust is applied to the stack.
- Dying and what follows: crash (crash spheres per body; Earth''s 100 km entry interface default or another value; contact speed rule near stations and asteroids), the Sun (pick the rule: hull temperature from `docs/architecture.md` "Collisions and the Sun" with warning and death thresholds, and the distance that gives in AU for the player''s ship), running out of life support if any; what the player sees (crash view, a message) and what follows (restart from the last checkpoint/save, or a fresh start); interaction with quests and saves (`milestone 5`).
- Saving: when (checkpoints at quest steps, docking, a key?), what (`saves.lisp` per `docs/architecture.md`); the milestone.
- Sound: what is heard (only what travels through the hull: engine rumble, RCS puffs, valves, alarms, radio static), synthesised per `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "Audio"; assign a milestone (architecture default 6) or cut.

### The cockpit
- Its DIY look: a welded frame inside the life-support module, mismatched screens and gauges bolted on, taped labels, exposed cable runs, a window and its frame; a layout drawing as a list of panels with positions (m, ship body axes) relative to the eye point, so the cockpit mesh can be built from it.
- Each instrument: what it shows, how it is drawn (an in-world screen rendered to a texture with Slug text, `text.lisp`; a needle gauge as a mesh; a lamp; a dot-matrix display; a small orbit plot on a screen), its update rate, and its milestone.
- Ghostty terminal screen: keep (e.g. a ship computer''s text console or the radio log, with what it costs per `docs/luv-capabilities.md` "HUD and instruments") or cut, with the reason; if kept, a decision extending `decision 3: luv ASDF systems the game depends on` is needed later.
- Interaction: switches pressed with the mouse or keys; whether the player can fly from the cockpit alone (the `milestone 3` outcome) and what the outside view adds.
- Readability rules: minimum text size at 1280x800, contrast in sunlight and shadow, colour use.
- How messages and radio reach the player (screens, a printer strip, a lamp; the story uses these).

### The places
- Earth: the Blue Marble (`decision 13`), atmosphere rim, night side (Black Marble or cut), clouds (cut or not), what can be seen from LEO; atmospheric drag: cut or an exponential atmosphere as a force (`decision 17` lists it as the design''s option), with the reason.
- Earth-orbit points of interest (`milestone 3` presets): each with name, purpose (station, propellant depot, debris field, a captured asteroid being moved, a satellite to service), orbit (a, e, i, Ω, ω or altitude/inclination, epoch; real where a real object exists, e.g. the ISS''s orbit from a named TLE source and date, otherwise plausible and stated as invented), Δv from the start orbit, and how it looks; 4 to 6 places so the autopilot test of `milestone 3` stays small.
- The Moon: texture source and licence (e.g. NASA/USGS LRO mosaic; checked per `decision 13`), lunar orbits of interest, landing: in or cut (the ship design decides; a tug without landing gear says cut), Δv for TLI, LOI.
- The asteroid belt and near-Earth asteroids: which real objects (e.g. a small NEA with known elements from JPL SBDB, named with designation and the date read), how they are shown (instanced meshes near, markers far, the belt not as a dense field), what work the ship does there (moving a small asteroid); Δv and travel times by Hohmann for the ship.
- Planets in scope: choose (e.g. Venus and Mars as flyby/transfer destinations, outer planets shown on the map only, or as designed) with travel times and Δv that the ship can do; their look (flat albedo impostors or textures with sources).
- The Sun: its look (impostor, bloom, glare per "Sun glare and brightness"), the danger zone in AU from the chosen heat rule, warnings.
- Real orbital data: list sources and the date read; game time stays before 2050-01-01 (`decision 18`).

### Scope
- A prioritised feature list as one table: feature, priority (must/should/could), milestone (2 flight core, 3 cockpit and autopilot, 4 the solar system, 5 story and quests, 6 release) or "cut", reason; covering every feature named above and in `milestone 1`''s developer ideas (rugged ships, low-tech weapons, autopilot presets, Moon, belt, planets, Sun death, DIY cockpit, story), plus title screen, intro, controls help, credits screen (`decision 13`), README, performance target, saves, audio, gamepad.
- Weapons: low-tech weapons only if the story needs them; decide in or cut here (`docs/story.md` follows).
- Consistent with the milestones'' Outcomes and Done-whens (e.g. `milestone 2` needs the outside view, the minimal readout and crash into Earth; `milestone 4` needs Sun death tested; `milestone 5` needs saves); where a milestone''s text and the design disagree, the design wins inside the milestone''s intent, and a real contradiction is a `pm decision add`.
- What `docs/story.md` may use, by milestone: a short list for `task 12: write plan: story`.

## Decisions to record (`pm decision record`)
- Ship and propulsion: the player''s ship (parts, masses), main engine and RCS technology with Isp and thrust, propellant, Δv budget.
- How death works: crash rules and radii, the Sun''s heat rule and thresholds, what follows (restart/checkpoint).
- Places in scope: Earth-orbit points of interest, the Moon (landing in or out), which asteroids, which planets.
- Docking or grappling, towing; damage model (or none).
- Control scheme and views; warp thrust limit and drop-out choices within `decision 19`.
- Cockpit instrument technology (Slug screens, Ghostty in or out); audio''s milestone; gamepad''s milestone; drag in or out; weapons in or out.
- Supersede an architecture decision only with a reason, and then update `docs/architecture.md` in the same task.

## Done when
- `docs/design.md` covers the sections above, agrees with `docs/luv-capabilities.md` and `docs/architecture.md`, and cites the decisions it relies on.
- Every feature is in the scope table with a milestone 2 to 6 or cut with a reason; every destination in scope has its Δv checked against the ship''s.
- The decisions above are recorded; follow-ups the document reveals (e.g. an asset whose licence must be checked, a wishlist item, an architecture section to adjust) are added as tasks in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- The ship(s): parts, propulsion, masses, inertia, Δv, looks; decisions recorded.
- Flight: controls, views, instruments, warp choices, autopilot, docking/towing, death, saving, sound; decisions recorded.
- The cockpit: layout, instruments and how each is drawn, messages.
- The places: Earth, points of interest, Moon, asteroids, planets, Sun, with data and sources and Δv checked against the ship; decisions recorded.
- Scope table and read-through against `docs/luv-capabilities.md`, `docs/architecture.md` and the milestones; the list for the story.', '2026-10-14T17:57:23Z', 11);
INSERT INTO plan (id, milestone_id, subject, priority, body, created_at, created_by_task) VALUES (11, 1, 'story', 500, 'Part 5 of `plan 1: masterplan`: write `docs/story.md`, the setting, characters, premise and linear quest line of the prelude. It runs after `plan 10: game design` and builds on what `docs/design.md` "Scope", "What the story may use" puts in milestones 2 to 5. `milestone 5: Story and quests: the playable prelude` implements the document (`quests.lisp`, RADIO/JOB, static texts, saves, a headless play-through), and `milestone 6: Release: one command to play, a README for people` takes its short introduction from it, so it must be specific enough to implement and to test from.

## Ground rules
- Read first: `docs/design.md` (all of it, above all "The game in one page", "The places", "Messages and radio", "Dying and what follows", "Scope"), `docs/architecture.md` ("Files in load order": `quests.lisp`, `saves.lisp`; "Scripted runs"; "Rails"; "Re-entry of objects"), `AGENTS.md`, `pm milestone show 5` and `pm decision list --answered`.
- Binding, cited, not reopened: `decision 22: The player''s ship and its propulsion`, `decision 24: Grapple, not docking; towing in`, `decision 25: Damage model`, `decision 26: Refuelling and economy`, `decision 27: Other ships`, `decision 33: Grapple tolerances, contact and berthing`, `decision 34: How death works`, `decision 35: Saving and audio`, `decision 37: Message channels the story may use`, `decision 38: Start orbit: polar`, `decision 39: Places in scope`, `decision 41: Weapons: cut`, `decision 42: Scope: what each milestone builds`.
- Only features `docs/design.md` assigns to milestones 2 to 5; nothing cut (weapons, fights, landing, money, EVA, docking ports, voices, faces, reply choices, destinations beyond the Moon and the passing rock). A quest built on a "should" (the passing rock, story ships moving) says so and names what the quest line does if it is dropped (the quest is skipped or replaced, the line stays complete).
- Hard sci-fi, early 2040s, all technology known: no aliens, no conspiracy tech, no secret drives. Danger comes from the trade (a spinning derelict, a heavy load, a long transfer, the Sun) and people''s conflicts told by RADIO (`docs/design.md` "Weapons").
- Every quest flyable by the Mule: its Δv within the propellant the pilot can have at that point (6.65 km/s empty, 1.74 km/s with 50 t, 1.02 km/s with 100 t; refuelling only berthed at Fulmar Depot, Highport, Peary Depot), its duration within 180 person-days with restocking at Tern Station, Highport or Peary Depot; take the figures from `docs/design.md` "Δv for the standard legs", "The places", "Δv checked against the ship", don''t recompute them unless a quest needs a leg not listed (then state the formula).
- Names: the story may rename the places (working names, `decision 39`); if it does, the same task updates `docs/design.md` (and `docs/architecture.md` where named) in step and records the renaming.
- The document is the story and the quest specification; no game code. Style per `AGENTS.md`: intro paragraph under each top-level heading, then concise bullets; a table for the quest line.

## docs/story.md: contents
### Setting
- The world of the early 2040s as the game shows it: who works in near-Earth space (the polar-plane economy of `docs/design.md` "The places": Tern Station, Fulmar Depot, Highport and its water plant, Skua Yard, Peary Depot), who owns what, what a tug pilot''s life is like; plausible, small, sober.
- The start date (`*start-date*`, `decision 14: Units and time epoch`; default 2040-01-01 00:00 UTC, before 2050-01-01 by `decision 18: Ephemerides`): chosen so the quest line''s windows work (the Moon''s best month from Highport''s perigee, `docs/design.md` "The Moon"; the passing rock''s perigee date and designation, "Asteroids"); the start orbit''s phase if the story changes it (`docs/design.md` "The start").
### Characters
- The pilot (the player; no gender or face needed, nothing shown but text addressed to them), the ship''s name and its previous owner if any, the employer(s), the people at the places who speak by RADIO (each with a call sign, a role, a voice in text, where they are, so the light-time delay is right).
- Other ships the story names (from `decision 27`: Mule tugs in other liveries, the tanker, the crew capsule, the rock mover), which are scenery and which move between places on rails (milestone 5 "should").
- Static texts: the ship''s name above the window and up to three tape notes (exact wording, `decision 37`).
### Premise
- Why the pilot is in this orbit with this tug, what they want, what goes on around them, and where the prelude ends: an ending that opens the longer adventure (a hook, not a resolution) without needing anything cut.
- The short introduction for `milestone 6` (a few sentences, exact text, shown before the first flight).
### The quest line
- 5 to 10 quests in a fixed order that teach piloting one step at a time, from manual attitude and a small burn in the start orbit, through rendezvous, grapple, berthing and refuelling, the derelict K-17 pushed to re-entry by hand (`docs/design.md` "The derelict''s re-entry"), towing a Skua Yard rock, the trip to Highport, a lunar transfer to Peary Depot, to the passing rock if built and a close call with the Sun''s warning zone only if it fits the Δv and life support (otherwise the Sun stays a danger told about, not a destination).
- Per quest: title, giver (RADIO sender), goal, place(s), skills taught (which controls, holds, autopilot presets, instruments), the milestone that makes it playable (2 to 5), the propellant and time it needs from the expected state at its start, its steps (each a JOB card: title and up to three lines; which steps need Y), the RADIO messages with their exact text, sent at which trigger, and the completion condition per step.
- Completion conditions machine-checkable from the simulation state (`docs/architecture.md`: berthed at a fixture, grappled object id, orbit elements within tolerances, an object removed with its cause, a body''s sphere entered, time elapsed), so `quests.lisp` and the headless play-through of `milestone 5` can test them; no condition on what the player reads or looks at, except Y acknowledgements.
- Failure: death never fails a quest; its step is flown again from the last checkpoint (`decision 34`); say where each quest takes a checkpoint (at quest steps, `decision 35`). A step that can be made impossible (e.g. a rock pushed away, propellant spent far from a depot) says how the game recovers (the step''s objects reset on restart from its checkpoint, a tanker or the employer''s offer by RADIO), without new mechanics.
- No quest needs a feature cut or not yet built at its milestone; the autopilot never flies what `docs/design.md` says it refuses (the derelict''s push below 150 km is by hand).
### How the story is told
- Which channel carries what (RADIO, JOB, SHIP stays the game''s, static texts), the tone of the radio (terse, working people, numbers where pilots would say them), what the COMMS screen shows between quests, how light time shows at the Moon.
- What the player sees of other ships at their work (scenery at places, departures and arrivals on rails out of view).
### Names and terms
- One list of every name the story fixes (characters, call signs, ship, company, places, the passing rock''s designation), so code (`places.lisp`, `quests.lisp`) and the other docs use them consistently.

## Decisions to record (`pm decision record`)
- Setting and premise (the pilot''s situation, the employer, where the prelude ends).
- Names: the ship, the characters and call signs, the companies, the places (renamed or kept), the passing rock''s designation.
- The start date (and the start orbit''s phase if changed).
- The quest line: its quests in order, each with its milestone; what happens if a "should" it relies on is dropped.

## Done when
- `docs/story.md` has the sections above, 5 to 10 quests each with every per-quest item, and uses only features `docs/design.md` assigns to milestones 2 to 5.
- Every quest''s Δv and duration checked against the ship''s propellant, refuelling and 180 person-days, citing `docs/design.md`.
- `docs/design.md` and `docs/architecture.md` agree with the names and the start date the story fixes; the decisions above are recorded.
- Follow-ups the story reveals (e.g. a Δv leg the design lacks, a place detail, a flight script `milestone 5` needs) added as tasks in the plan they belong to.

## Suggested split
- Setting, characters, premise, the start date and the names; decisions recorded; `docs/design.md` and `docs/architecture.md` updated for renamings and the date.
- The quest line: quests, steps, completion conditions, Δv and time checks, checkpoints; decision recorded.
- How the story is told: every RADIO message and JOB card in exact text, the tape notes, the introduction; a read-through of `docs/story.md` against `docs/design.md` "What the story may use".', '2026-10-14T19:33:18Z', 12);

